WEBVTT
TITLE Counting Linear Extensions — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Four tasks and three constraints: A before B, A before C, and both B and C before D.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.000
A B C D is legal: read every arrow against the ordering and each one still points forward.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:23.000
B A C D is not legal. The constraint A before B is broken, so this ordering is not a linear extension.

4
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:30.000
A linear extension is a full ordering in which every constraint still points forward. This diamond has exactly two.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Two elements with no constraint: A B and B A, so two orderings. Add A before B and only one is left.

6
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Three elements with no constraint give six. With A first, two. As a chain, one.

7
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:52.000
The count always lies between 1 and n factorial, and both ends are reached.

8
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Adding a constraint can only lower the count. It can never raise it, and it may leave it unchanged.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Four elements, three constraints: A before C, B before C, B before D. Twenty-four orderings exist.

10
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Test each one. C A B D fails, because C comes before B and the constraint says B before C.

11
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Five survive: A B C D, A B D C, B A C D, B A D C and B D A C.

12
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Twenty-four orderings tested, five accepted. That is the definition, and it is also a method.

13
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Two blocks in series: every element of the first comes before every element of the second. The counts multiply.

14
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Two blocks in parallel: nothing crosses between them, so their elements interleave. Choose which slots belong to the first block.

15
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Sizes m and n: the number of ways to choose the m slots is C of m plus n, choose m.

16
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Series-parallel posets are exactly the ones these two rules reach. Every closed form in this lesson is built from them.

17
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Two chains of two: B before C, and D before E. Four slots between them, and two slots go to the first chain.

18
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Six ways: slots one and two, one and three, one and four, two and three, two and four, three and four.

19
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.000
Sizes three and two give C of five choose two, which is ten. The smaller chain takes the chosen slots.

20
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.000
The binomial is not a division by two. It counts the choices of position, and positions are what the two chains compete for.

21
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Three chains of two: choose two slots of six, then two of four, then two of two. Fifteen times six times one.

22
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:48.000
A common shortcut divides n factorial by two for every constraint. Here it happens to give 720 over 8, which is 90.

23
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:55.000
On one chain of three it gives six over four, which is 1.5. A count is never a fraction.

24
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.000
On a chain of four it gives twenty-four over eight, which is three, and the true answer is one.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:08.000
A rooted tree read downwards is a partial order: each node after its parent. Write in each node its subtree size.

26
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:17.000
Hook length: n factorial over the product of the subtree sizes. Twenty-four over eight is three.

27
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:25.000
A forest of two chains of two: sizes 2, 1, 2, 1, product four, so twenty-four over four is six.

28
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:30.000
The formula is exact for forests and for series-parallel posets. It fails the moment a poset is neither.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:38.000
A before C, B before C, B before D. No series join and no parallel join produces this poset.

30
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:46.000
Count by cases on the last element. If C is last, A and B and D are free except B before D: three orderings.

31
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:55.000
If D is last, C must follow both A and B, so C is last among the three: two orderings. Three plus two is five.

32
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:04.000
Brightwell and Winkler proved in 1991 that counting linear extensions is #P-complete. No formula exists for the general case.

33
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:12.000
The method that always works: let f of S be the number of ways to order the set S as a prefix of the sequence.

34
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:20.000
The next element must have all of its predecessors already in S. Sum f of S plus that element over every legal choice.

35
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:26.000
Four elements give sixteen subsets. Fill them in order of size; the full set counts one way and the empty set holds the answer.

36
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:32.000
The cost is n times two to the n. That is fine to about twenty elements, which is why questions keep n small or the shape special.

37
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:40.000
GATE CSE 2016, Set 1, Question 11: the number of different topological orderings of the given graph.

38
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:48.000
A is the only source and F the only sink, so every ordering starts with A and ends with F. Four slots remain.

39
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.000
The middle is two chains in parallel: B before C, and D before E. So C of four choose two, which is six.

40
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
The six: A B C D E F, A B D C E F, A B D E C F, A D B C E F, A D B E C F, A D E B C F. The answer is 6.

41
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:08.000
Two threads, two statements each. The six interleavings are the linear extensions of two parallel chains of two.

42
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:17.000
GATE CSE 2024, Set 1, Question 40: all six orders are legal, but they produce only three distinct value pairs.

43
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:26.000
GATE CSE 2017, Set 2, Question 44: eight operations give C of eight choose four, seventy schedules; sixteen create a cycle, leaving fifty-four.

44
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:36.000
One arithmetic, three names: topological orderings, thread interleavings, serialisable schedules. Count the orderings that respect the constraints.

